WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:04.470
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:09.890
This is the first paper in Two Minute Papers
that showcased so stunning results that people

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:12.830
called it out to be an April Fools' day joke.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:17.279
It is based on a deep neural network, and
the concept is very simple: you choose an

00:00:17.279 --> 00:00:23.199
artistic style, you make a terrible drawing,
and it creates a beautiful painting out of it.

00:00:23.199 --> 00:00:27.099
If you would like to know more about deep
neural networks, we've had a ton of fun

00:00:27.099 --> 00:00:31.050
with them in previous episodes, I've put a
link to them in the description box!

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:36.360
I expect an onslaught of magnificent results
with this technique to appear very soon.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:41.370
It is important to note that one needs to
create a semantic map for each artistic style

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:47.210
so that the algorithm learns the correspondence
between the painting and the semantics. However,

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:52.480
these maps have to be created only once and
can be used forever, so I expect quite a few

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.420
of them to show up in the near future, which
greatly simplifies the workflow.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:01.640
After that, these annotations can be changed
at will, you press a button, and the rest

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.280
is history.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:24.470
Whoa! Wicked results.

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:29.740
Some of these neural art results are so good
that we should be creating a new class of

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:35.040
Turing tests for paintings. This means that
we are presented with two images, one of them

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.400
is painted by a human, and one by a computer.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:43.890
We need to click the ones that we think were
painted by a human.

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:45.970
Damn, curses!

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:51.229
As always, these techniques are new and heavily
experimental, and this usually means that

00:01:51.229 --> 00:01:55.830
they take quite a bit of time to compute.
The presentation video you have seen was sped

00:01:55.830 --> 00:02:00.720
up considerably. If these works are worthy
of further attention, and I definitely think

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:06.140
they are, then we can expect great strides
towards interactivity in followup papers very

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:11.080
soon. I am really looking forward to it and
we Fellow Scholars will have a ton of fun

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:12.730
with these tools in the future.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:16.730
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

